        {
          "row": 6,
          "what": "The plate shows the log jail's own log walls and roof standing well clear above the fence, left of the keeper house. In this render the core is concealed behind the palisade and almost none of it reads. A visitor comparing the two would notice the jail itself is missing from the picture's dominant position: the palisade is too high for the core against this picture, or the core too low, and this increment resolves neither.",
          "status": "open"
        },
        {
          "row": 7,
          "what": "The compound's relative proportions are wrong against the plate: the palisade fills most of this render's frame while the plate's fence is a secondary element behind a dominant building group. This follows from the uncalibrated diagnostic camera as much as from the geometry, and no metric error is claimed from it.",
          "status": "open"
        },
        {
          "row": 8,
          "what": "The keeper house's drawn elevation is the north front and east gable of the reconstructed `keeper_gale_1902` layout. Whether the plate's house is the same building seen the same way is not established \u2014 the plate is captioned 1836 and Andreas draws a different elevation entirely.",
          "status": "open"
        },
        {
          "row": 9,
          "what": "Tone and material: the render's palisade reads as one flat dark brown mass, where the plate draws light vertical boards with visible joints and shadow. No board-by-board palisade stock is modelled and none is claimed.",
          "status": "open"
